The defining condition says exactly that every event of the form ${X <= x}$ is assigned a probability, since $cal(F)$ contains it. More generally, for $A subset.eq RR$ the notation
$
  {X in A} = {omega in Omega: X(omega) in A}
$
identifies a subset of the sample space, so statements such as $PP(X <= x)$ or $PP(X in A)$ are ordinary probabilities of events.
